  "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x-1}{(x+1)(2x-3)}=\\frac{A}{x+1}+\\frac{B}{2x-3}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x+1)(2x-3)$ gives\n\n$$\n4x-1=A(2x-3)+B(x+1).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-1$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-1)-1 & =A(2(-1)-3)+B(-1+1) \\\\ -5 & =-5A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(\\frac{3}{2})-1 & =A(2(\\frac{3}{2})-3)+B(\\frac{3}{2}+1) \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{5}{2}B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x-1}{(x+1)(2x-3)}=\\frac{1}{x+1}+\\frac{2}{2x-3}.\n$$",
